{\bkmkstart AAAAAABFZY}
{\bkmkend AAAAAABFZY}
{
\pard\plain \s61\li360\sa60\sb30\qj\widctlpar\qj\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
Vertex normals. The array contains normalized vectors, nullptr if not present. The array is mNumVertices in size. Normals are undefined for point and line primitives. A mesh consisting of points and lines only may not have normal vectors. Meshes with mixed primitive types (i.e. lines and triangles) may have normals, but the normals for vertices that are only referenced by point or line primitives are undefined and set to QNaN (WARN: qNaN compares to inequal to {\i everything} , even to qNaN itself. Using code like this to check whether a field is qnan is: {
\par
\pard\plain \s41\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \shading1000\cbpat8 \f2\fs16\cgrid {\cf21 #define IS_QNAN(f) (f != f)}\par
}
 still dangerous because even 1.f == 1.f could evaluate to false! ( remember the subtleties of IEEE754 artithmetics). Use stuff like {\f2 fpclassify}  instead. \par
{{\s5\sb90\sa30\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs16\cgrid 
Note\par}\pard\plain \s82\li720\widctlpar\ql\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\s17 \sa60 \sb30
Normal vectors computed by {\b Assimp} are always unit-length. However, this needn't apply for normals that have been taken directly from the model file. \par
}}}}
